<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aorthogonality-is-a-vanishing-pairing> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Identical invariant, and both books use the self-orthogonality fact for the same purpose -- as the step that forces a difference to vanish (Apostol in the uniqueness half of Theorem 15.15, Strang in showing that two orthogonal subspaces meet only at 0, and again in showing that x_r is determined by Ax). The divergence is in where the fact is grounded. Apostol derives it from the positivity axiom, so it holds in any Euclidean space including C(a,b): a continuous function orthogonal to itself is the zero function. Strang states it as a property of vectors in R^n, obtained from v-transpose-v = 0. His version is a special case, and because it is stated for vectors rather than for the pairing, the transfer to functions is not available to him inside these sections." .
