<http://studium.local/node/d%3Aexample-11-a-nonzero-prescribed-value-breaks-closure> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Apostol notes that the number 0 is essential and that prescribing a nonzero value c violates the closure axioms; concretely, f(1) = g(1) = c gives (f+g)(1) = 2c, which differs from c when c is nonzero, and 0f has value 0 rather than c. The zero on the right-hand side of the constraint is what makes the condition survive addition and scaling." .
