<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aorthogonality-to-a-whole-set> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol defines S-perp for an *arbitrary subset* S and states that it is a subspace 'whether or not S itself is one', leaving the verification as a simple exercise rather than proving it; that generality is what licenses him to form the perp of a mere list of generators, which is the step the proof in 15.15 needs (he checks orthogonality against basis elements only, then upgrades to the span). Strang defines the perp only of a subspace V, and does so after he already knows the dimension count, so his perp always arrives paired with its partner in a complement pair. Strang's concrete gain is large -- 'the nullspace consists of the vectors perpendicular to the rows' is literally the equation Ax = 0 read row by row, which no abstract statement can give -- but he cannot form the perp of a generating list as a separate object, and he needs r + (n - r) = n, i.e. elimination, to know that the perp is exactly the row space and not something larger." .
<http://studium.local/node/d%3Adegree-exactly-n-is-not-a-subspace-of-degree-at-most-n/anchor/0> <http://studium.local/p/quote> "The set of all polynomials of degree equal to n is not a linear space because the closure axioms are not satisfied." .
