<http://studium.local/node/d%3Aparseval-conjugate-comes-from-hermitian-symmetry> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Tracing the conjugate precisely: pairing (15.10) with y in the first slot gives (x, y) = sum of (x, e_i)(e_i, y), where each c_i = (x, e_i) is extracted by homogeneity in the FIRST slot and no conjugate arises. The conjugate appears only at the last step, when (e_i, y) is rewritten as the conjugate of (y, e_i) by axiom (1'), so that the formula is expressed in the components of y rather than in reversed products. So (15.11)'s conjugate is a bookkeeping consequence of Hermitian symmetry, not of conjugate-homogeneity." .
