<http://studium.local/node/d%3A15-10-nonzero-hypothesis-is-essential> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Theorem 15.10 asserts independence for every orthogonal set OF NONZERO ELEMENTS, and the restriction is genuinely needed, not cosmetic. Adjoining O to an orthogonal set leaves it orthogonal, because the condition constrains only pairs of distinct elements and O is orthogonal to everything; but the enlarged set is dependent, so without the nonzero hypothesis the theorem would be false. Containing O is not by itself sufficient for orthogonality: {O, i, i + j} contains O and is dependent, yet is not an orthogonal set. Inside the proof the hypothesis is used at exactly one point, to divide by (x_1, x_1)." .
