<http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "For elements x and y of a Euclidean space with y not the zero element, the element ((x, y)/(y, y)) y is called the projection of x along y. The Gram-Schmidt step forms y_{r+1} by subtracting from x_{r+1} its projection along each earlier y_i. In 15.14 the same notion is applied to an orthonormal basis element, where (y, y) = 1 reduces the coefficient to (x, e_i)." .
<http://studium.local/edge/674> <http://studium.local/p/evFile> "15.08.md" .
<http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Acx-sampling-inner-product-on-p-n> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Exercise 10 defines (f, g) as the sum over k = 0, ..., n of f(k/n) g(k/n) on the space P_n of polynomials of degree at most n. Positivity holds precisely because a nonzero polynomial of degree at most n cannot vanish at all n + 1 sample points. On the space of all real polynomials the same formula is degenerate: the product of the n + 1 factors (t - k/n) is nonzero yet has vanishing self-pairing. Whether the formula is an inner product depends on the dimension of the space it is placed on." .
