<http://studium.local/node/x%3Alinear-independence> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol defines dependence first and independence as its negation, over an unordered and possibly infinite *set*; the distinctness and finiteness clauses are therefore load-bearing, and the definition applies verbatim to {1, t, t^2, ...} and to exponentials with distinct exponents. Strang defines independence first, for a finite *sequence*, and in the same breath converts it into the matrix test N(A) = {0}, i.e. into elimination. The trade is a decision procedure against a domain: Strang can settle independence of any concrete list in finitely many row operations and can never phrase the question for an infinite family; Apostol can phrase it for infinite families and must invent a fresh ad hoc argument (differentiate and evaluate at 0; a limit argument for exponentials) for each one, since he has no algorithm at all." .
