<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aorthogonal-subspaces-versus-complements> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "The shared invariant is only the naming rule plus exhaustion. The *contrast* between mutual orthogonality and complementarity is Strang-only: he isolates it as an explicit trap and spends a counterexample on it (two walls of a room look perpendicular but are not orthogonal subspaces; two planes in R^3 can never be orthogonal complements, because the dimensions must add up), and he can make the point sharply precisely because he has dimension arithmetic. The weaker relation therefore exists as a named object for him -- orthogonal subspaces, whose only forced consequence is that they meet in the zero vector -- and not for Apostol. On Apostol's route the distinction cannot arise: he only ever forms S-perp of a given S, and S together with S-perp is a complement pair by construction (Theorem 15.15), so he needs neither the name nor the warning. Apostol pays with a silent gap -- a reader of 15.14 alone gets no warning that 'mutually orthogonal' is weaker than 'complementary' -- while Strang pays by needing the finite-dimensional count to say anything at all." .
