<http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Acx-inhomogeneous-endpoint-condition-breaks-closure> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "The set of real-valued functions with domain containing 0 and 1 that satisfy f(1) = 1 + f(0), with the usual pointwise addition and scaling, is not a linear space: Axiom 1 fails because f(x) = x and g(x) = x both lie in the set while (f + g)(1) = 2 but 1 + (f + g)(0) = 1, and Axiom 2 fails because (2f)(1) = 2 but 1 + (2f)(0) = 1. Axiom 5 also fails, since the zero function gives 0 on the left and 1 on the right. The neighbouring Exercises 3 and 4 impose homogeneous conditions on the same two values and are linear spaces, so the whole failure is caused by the additive constant 1." .
