<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aall-bases-are-equinumerous> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Same statement, and both prove it by a two-sided counting squeeze, but the engine differs. Apostol routes it through Theorem 15.5 ('k+1 elements of the span of an independent k-set are dependent'), a pure consequence of the axioms, applied twice by symmetry. Strang writes the w's as combinations of the v's, i.e. as a matrix product VA with A short-and-wide, and then quotes his own 'm < n forces dependent columns'. Strang's proof is shorter and constructive but it is a proof *about matrices*: it presupposes that the vectors can be stacked into columns, so as written it does not cover matrix spaces or function spaces -- for which he only asserts the analogue. Apostol's proof needs no representation and therefore certifies dim of the polynomials of degree <= n directly." .
