<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aorthogonal-decomposition-of-the-ambient-space> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Same theorem, incompatible hypotheses. Apostol needs only that S be finite-dimensional -- the ambient V may be infinite-dimensional, which is exactly what lets him apply the theorem to C(0, 2pi) with S a space of trigonometric polynomials. His existence proof runs through a finite orthonormal basis of S, so Gram-Schmidt (15.13) is a genuine prerequisite. Strang works in R^n and gets the splitting from Part 2 of the Fundamental Theorem, which he obtains from the dimension count r + (n - r) = n produced by elimination; no orthonormal basis is constructed anywhere. Strang's version therefore yields something Apostol's does not -- that A restricted from row space to column space is invertible, the germ of the pseudoinverse -- while Apostol's version applies where Strang's count is unavailable." .
