<http://studium.local/node/d%3Anorm-homogeneity-property> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Theorem 15.9(c): ||cx|| = |c| ||x|| for every scalar c. Its proof uses homogeneity in the first slot to pull c out, then symmetry (or (1') in the complex case) plus homogeneity again to pull the second copy out, giving (cx, cx) = c times conjugate(c) times (x, x) = |c|^2 (x, x). The absolute value, and in the complex case the modulus, is produced by this second extraction, which is why the identity reads |c| and not c." .
