<http://studium.local/node/d%3Ainner-product-not-unique-on-a-given-space> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Example 2 exhibits on V_2 the form (x, y) = 2x_1y_1 + x_1y_2 + x_2y_1 + x_2y_2, which satisfies all the axioms yet differs from the dot product. Apostol draws the conclusion explicitly: there may be more than one inner product in a given linear space. Hence 'Euclidean space' is a linear space together with a chosen inner product, not a property of the linear space alone." .
