<http://studium.local/node/d%3A15-10-n-orthogonal-nonzero-elements-form-a-basis> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "The second clause of Theorem 15.10: in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with dim V = n, every orthogonal set consisting of n nonzero elements is a basis for V. Apostol's proof is a two-step composition, independence from the first clause, then Theorem 15.7(b) to upgrade n independent elements in an n-dimensional space to a basis. So this clause adds no inner-product content; its whole force comes from a dimension-counting theorem proved earlier." .
