<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aright-count-upgrades-one-basis-property-to-both> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol states it as Theorem 15.7(b) for an abstract space of dimension n and delegates the proof to Chapter 12, so on his route it is a labour-saving corollary of the counting theory and nothing more. Strang states both halves (n independent vectors span; n spanning vectors are independent), notes that it holds in any vector space, and then immediately re-reads it as a statement about an n by n matrix being invertible -- which converts an existence claim into a determinant/elimination test. That conversion is the gain and the limit at once: it is a decision procedure in R^n, and it is unavailable for the matrix and function spaces he has just admitted, where his own general formulation still holds but cannot be checked by his methods. Apostol's version, by contrast, applies to a space of polynomials verbatim, and he uses it in 15.11 to conclude that n nonzero orthogonal elements are a basis." .
