<http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Acx-a-basis-for-v-need-not-contain-a-basis-for-a-subspace> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Exercise 24(c) states that every basis for a subspace S extends to a basis for V, and Exercise 24(d) denies the converse: in V = V_2 let S be the line spanned by (1, 1), and take the basis {(1, 0), (0, 1)} for V. No nonempty subset of that basis is a basis for S, because neither (1, 0) nor (0, 1) lies on the line x = y, and the subsets of size 2 span V and not S. The asymmetry is that extension is a choice one is free to make while a prescribed basis has already made it wrongly." .
