<http://studium.local/node/d%3Aexponential-weight-makes-improper-integral-converge> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Example 5 puts (f, g) = integral from 0 to infinity of e^(-t) f(t) g(t) dt on the space of all real polynomials. Apostol points out that the exponential factor is what makes this improper integral converge for every choice of polynomials. Here the weight does double duty: positivity gives axiom (4), and decay gives well-definedness of the value at all, which on an unbounded interval is a prerequisite for the axioms even to be checkable." .
