<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aleast-squares-is-a-projection> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "The two books arrive from opposite motivations. Apostol's motive is APPROXIMATION THEORY: x is a continuous function, S is a space of polynomials or trigonometric polynomials, and the question 'which element of S is nearest' is posed for its own sake, with no equation in sight. Strang's motive is an UNSOLVABLE SYSTEM: b lies outside the column space, so Ax = b has no solution, and the least-squares problem is the salvage operation -- his 4.1 announces the error vector b - Ax as living in N(A-transpose) before projections are even defined. Consequence: Apostol never mentions equations, so a reader of ch15 alone would not know that this theory solves overdetermined systems; Strang, inside 4.1-4.2, has no norm on functions and hence no way to pose approximation of a function, so the entire polynomial/Fourier half of the subject is invisible from his side of the alignment." .
