<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aunique-coordinates-in-a-basis> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Here the two books are closest: even the proofs coincide line for line (assume two expansions, subtract, invoke independence). The only divergence is what the theorem is *for*. Apostol uses it to define the coordinate n-tuple, i.e. to justify the very notion of components, and immediately notes that the tuple depends on the ordering and not only on the basis-as-a-set. Strang states it in one line and does not build coordinates on it, because his vectors already arrive as coordinate tuples in R^n -- the theorem he actually needs later is not 'coordinates exist' but 'A is invertible from row space to column space'. So the theorem that founds Apostol's coordinates is, for Strang, a remark he can afford to spend two sentences on." .
