<http://studium.local/node/d%3Aorthogonal-decomposition-existence> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Exhibiting a decomposition splits into four obligations that are discharged separately and rest on different prior results: obtain a finite orthonormal basis of S (this is where finite-dimensionality of S is consumed, via Gram-Schmidt); define s as the sum of the projections of x along that basis; check s lies in S (it is a linear combination of basis elements); and check s perp = x - s is orthogonal to S, which itself needs the reduction from all of S to the basis elements. Only the second obligation is a construction; the other three are verifications, and the choice of basis is the only arbitrary input, which is why the uniqueness half, once proved, is what makes the construction independent of that choice." .
