<http://studium.local/edge/892> <http://studium.local/p/dst> <http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-set> .
<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aspan-of-a-set> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol builds the span bottom-up as an operator L(S) on arbitrary (possibly infinite) subsets, proves it is the smallest subspace containing S by the closure test, and must therefore also legislate L(empty) = {O}. Strang has the concept but no operator and no notation: 'span' is a verb applied to a finite list, and the object it produces is always already named as something else -- the column space or the row space of a matrix, or the solution space of an ODE. He can and does handle non-column spans (in 3.4 he names {sin x, cos x} as a basis of the solutions of y'' = -y), so the loss is not expressive power over such objects but the absence of a *term for the operation*: there is no L(S) to write down, no way to name a subspace by its generators alone before knowing what it turns out to be, and hence no way to state 'different sets may span the same subspace' as a fact about an operator being non-injective. What Strang buys instead is decidability: the pivot columns of A are a basis for C(A), computed by elimination. He also loses Apostol's empty-set convention and has to reinstate the zero space by hand, declaring the empty set a basis for Z in the last paragraph of 3.4." .
<http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Acx-a-basis-for-v-need-not-contain-a-basis-for-a-subspace> <http://studium.local/p/name_en> "A basis for the whole space need not contain a basis for a given subspace" .
