<http://studium.local/node/x%3Asolution-set-of-a-homogeneous-linear-condition> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol exhibits this as one example among twelve, for a second-order homogeneous differential equation, and notes only that the zero on the right-hand side is essential -- the inhomogeneous set fails the closure axioms. He gets the differential-equation case into the same theory as R^n for free, and gets no method: he asserts that the solution space has dimension 2 without solving anything. Strang makes exactly this decomposition the goal of section 3.3 and supplies the algorithm: eliminate on the augmented matrix, read the particular solution off d, read the n - r special solutions off the free columns. He gains a complete procedure for matrices and pays by never once applying it to a differential equation, so the shared structure -- that both are the same coset statement -- is left for the reader to notice." .
