<http://studium.local/node/x%3Avectors-need-not-be-columns> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol makes this the *starting point*: the ten axioms come first, V_n is only EXAMPLE 3 of twelve in 15.03, and function spaces (EXAMPLE 5-10: all functions, polynomials, C(a,b), differentiable, integrable) sit on the same page as R^n, so every later theorem is automatically a theorem about functions. Strang gets to the same place from the other end and does get there in full: 3.4 runs on columns, elimination and pivots for its first two thirds, then a named section 'Bases for Matrix Spaces and Function Spaces' actually computes non-column dimensions -- 2x2 matrices (4, with an explicit basis), n by n matrices (n^2), upper triangular (n^2/2 + n/2), diagonal (n), symmetric (n^2/2 + n/2) -- and then bases of ODE solution spaces: {x, 1} for y'' = 0, {sin x, cos x} for y'' = -y, {e^x, e^-x} for y'' = y, dimension 2 each. So the divergence is not coverage but *order and leverage*. Apostol's generality is load-bearing (his approximation theory in 15.11-15.16 runs on C(a,b), never on R^n), whereas Strang's is terminal: the matrix machinery he built (pivots, rank, R) does not apply to sin x and cos x, so the non-column bases are read off by inspection and nothing downstream in Chapter 3 uses them. Apostol pays by having no algorithm for *producing* a basis or a dimension from given data anywhere in 15.05-15.08; his single constructive algorithm in the chapter is Gram-Schmidt (15.13), and that one orthogonalizes a sequence you already have." .
