<http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Acx-l2-sequence-space-is-an-infinite-dimensional-euclidean-space> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Exercise 13 takes V to be the set of real sequences {x_n} for which the series of squares converges, with (x, y) the sum of x_n y_n. It is a Euclidean space of infinite dimension, so the inner-product and norm machinery of 15.10 (Cauchy-Schwarz, the norm, the triangle inequality, the angle) and Theorem 15.10 of 15.11 apply with no finite-dimension hypothesis, while Theorems 15.11 and 15.12 of that section do assume finite dimension. The square-summability restriction is what makes the pairing finite; on the space of all real sequences the same formula diverges, for instance when every x_n = y_n = 1." .
