<http://studium.local/node/x%3Adimension-versus-rank> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol has dimension and no rank whatsoever: the word does not occur in Chapter 15. The chain 15.05-15.07 is metric-free and representation-free, and 'dim V = n' is all he ever needs, so he can assign a dimension to C(a,b)'s polynomial subspaces without computing anything. Strang has both, and the entire content of 3.5 is the bridge between them. That bridge is Strang-only and has no Apostol counterpart at all: the Rank Theorem (independent columns = independent rows), the Counting Theorem, and the Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra part 1 (all four subspace dimensions of one matrix at once) are statements about a matrix, so they are not shared invariants but Strang's own additions -- Apostol's vocabulary cannot formulate them, having no matrix and hence no four subspaces. What rank buys Strang is arithmetic: r + (n - r) = n pins those four dimensions simultaneously. What it costs is the confusion he then has to police explicitly, since a space has no rank and a matrix has no dimension." .
