<http://studium.local/node/d%3Aexample-11-functions-vanishing-at-a-point> <http://studium.local/p/name_zh> "例11:在 1 处有定义且取值为 0 的函数" .
<http://studium.local/node/d%3Alinear-space-is-a-set-together-with-two-operations> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "A linear space is not a set by itself but a set V paired with a rule for adding two elements and a rule for multiplying an element by a number. Specifying V without the two operations specifies nothing, and putting different operations on the same underlying set produces a different linear space." .
<http://studium.local/node/x%3Azero-element-destroys-independence> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Both books reach 'the empty set is a basis for the zero space', but from opposite directions. Apostol gets it for free and in the open: independence is a universally quantified statement, so it is vacuously true of the empty set (EXAMPLE 4 of 15.07), and L(empty) is declared to be {O}; no motive is needed. Strang gets it as a closing stipulation in the last paragraph of 3.4, and the only reason he gives is 'because then linear independence is lost'. That the deeper function of the rule is to protect the dimension count -- admitting O would let a space have bases of different sizes and break dim = number of basis vectors -- is substantive but *not stated by the textbook*; it is our inference, not his sentence. So a fact that is a triviality on the axiomatic route arrives on the matrix route as an unexplained house rule." .
<http://studium.local/node/d%3Athm-15-6-symmetry-step-requires-t-to-span> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "Interchanging S and T gives k at most m, but this reuse is legitimate only because T also spans V, so that Theorem 15.5 can be applied with T in the role of S. The phrase the same argument hides the second, and only, use of T being a spanning set." .
