<http://studium.local/node/apostol%3Acx-a-whole-line-of-right-zeros> <http://studium.local/p/statement> "In the structure of Exercise 31(c), where (x_1, x_2) + (y_1, y_2) = (x_1, x_2 + y_2), every element of the form (t, 0) satisfies x + (t, 0) = (x_1, x_2) for all x, so there are infinitely many zero elements and Theorem 15.1 fails. The proof of Theorem 15.1 gets O_1 + O_2 = O_1 and O_2 + O_1 = O_2 from Axiom 5 and then needs the commutative law to identify the two, and it is exactly Axiom 3 that fails here. Axiom 6 fails as well against any fixed choice of zero, since x + (-1)x = (x_1, 0) depends on x: for x = (1, 5) the sum is (1, 0), not (0, 0)." .
