<http://studium.local/node/x%3Athe-pairing-that-carries-geometry> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "Apostol axiomatises the pairing and then exhibits several on one and the same space -- the component sum, a weighted sum, an integral, a weighted integral -- so 'orthogonal' is always relative to a chosen product, and the sentence 'cos nx is orthogonal to sin mx' becomes literally the same sentence as 'e_1 is orthogonal to e_2'. Strang fixes one pairing and writes it as v-transpose-w, which is not a neutral choice: the transpose then does double duty as the adjoint, so A-transpose generates the other two of his four subspaces and A-transpose-A appears as the normal-equation matrix. That single notation is what makes his whole chapter computable, and it is also what hides the arbitrariness of the pairing: nothing in his notation suggests that an integral could take the place of the sum, and function approximation is therefore deferred out of the chapter entirely." .
<http://studium.local/node/d%3As-perp-is-orthogonal-to-each-basis-element/anchor/1> <http://studium.local/p/quote> "But from (15.18), we find that $(s, e_j) = (x, e_j)$ , so $s^\\perp$ is orthogonal to $e_j$ ." .
