<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aprojection-onto-a-one-dimensional-direction> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "The formula is character-for-character the same object -- coefficient = pairing with the direction over the direction's self-pairing. The roles are reversed. For Apostol this is an *internal step of an algorithm*: it is named only in passing while explaining Gram-Schmidt, and at that point he has not yet claimed it minimises any distance (minimality waits for 15.16). For Strang it is the *foundation of the chapter*: it is introduced as the closest-point problem, the error is shown perpendicular at once, and it is immediately packaged as a rank-one matrix P = a a-transpose over a-transpose-a with P squared equal to P. Strang thereby gains the operator viewpoint -- projection as a matrix acting on all b at once, and I - P projecting onto the perpendicular complement -- which Apostol never forms, since without a representation he has no object 'the projection map'." .
