<http://studium.local/node/x%3Abasis> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "The invariant is identical; the carrier is not. Apostol's basis is a *set*, so ordering is absent and must be added later as a separate definition (ordered basis) at exactly the point where components are introduced. Strang's basis is a *sequence* from the start, because he intends to load it into the columns of a matrix, and ordering is then free. Strang's cost shows up as a warning label: because bases and matrices are so nearly identified in his notation, he has to forbid the phrases 'the dimension of a basis' and 'the basis of a matrix' outright. Apostol's cost is confined to the existence chain 15.05-15.08: there he asserts that a basis exists (Theorem 15.7a) and never produces one, whereas every Strang basis in 3.4-3.5 comes out of elimination. It is not true that Apostol never constructs anything -- 15.13 gives Gram-Schmidt, his one algorithm in the chapter, and its corollary constructs an orthogonal basis of any finite-dimensional Euclidean space. The difference is that Gram-Schmidt improves a sequence you are handed, while elimination extracts a basis from a subspace presented as a column space or a nullspace." .
