<http://studium.local/node/x%3Aprojection-onto-a-subspace> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "This is the sharpest trade in the two chapters, and it is a trade of *hypothesis against computation*. Apostol defines the projection as the sum of the projections of x along the elements of an ORTHONORMAL basis of S. The definition is therefore unusable until such a basis exists, which is precisely why Gram-Schmidt and Theorem 15.14 must come first (15.13 before 15.14); in exchange the formula is a bare sum of pairings, requires no inversion, no determinant and no independence hypothesis beyond that of a basis, and it reads identically in C(a,b), where it becomes the Fourier/Legendre partial sum. Strang starts from an arbitrary INDEPENDENT list a_1,...,a_n -- no orthogonality assumed, hence no Gram-Schmidt needed at this point in the book -- and pays for it twice: he must impose orthogonality as n equations, A-transpose(b - A x-hat) = 0, and then SOLVE the resulting normal equation A-transpose-A x-hat = A-transpose-b, which forces him to prove separately that A-transpose-A is invertible exactly when the columns are independent. Net: Apostol front-loads the cost into constructing a basis and gets a closed formula; Strang front-loads nothing and pays with a linear system plus an invertibility theorem every single time. Strang's compensation is the projection matrix P = A(A-transpose-A)^{-1}A-transpose, an object valid for all b simultaneously and characterisable by P-transpose = P, P squared = P -- which Apostol's basis-dependent sum never becomes." .
