<http://studium.local/node/x%3Abest-approximation-by-the-projection> <http://studium.local/p/divergence> "For Apostol minimality is a THEOREM, stated separately in its own section 15.15, with a proof and an explicit uniqueness clause ('the equality sign holds if and only if t = s'); the projection was defined algebraically first, and its optimality had to be earned. For Strang minimality is the DEFINITION -- 'the closest vector p in S' is how projection onto a subspace is introduced in the section header -- so there is nothing left to prove, and no uniqueness clause is stated anywhere in 4.2. That is the whole cost of his choice: he never has to prove the approximation theorem, and he also never gets a statement of it, so within 4.1-4.2 the fact that the minimiser is unique is carried only by the geometry of the picture. The application layers then diverge completely: Apostol's theorem instantiates to Fourier partial sums and Legendre approximation of continuous functions, both of which are the same theorem with a different pairing; Strang's flows into least squares fitting of data, and he defers function approximation out of the chapter." .
